#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171821 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "f0111439-4fd8-5add-b95f-5e783178fd63" :question/math-academy-id "e-9440" :question/problem "Draw a diagram that represents the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j}$ at the points $(-2,1), (-1,-2)$, and $(1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "To sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\,\n$$\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathbf{F} ( - 2 , 1 ) & = - 2 ( - 2 ) ( 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + ( - 2 {)}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j} = 4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathbf{F} ( - 1 , - 2 ) & = - 2 ( - 1 ) ( - 2 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + ( - 1 {)}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j} = - 4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathbf{F} ( 1 , - 1 ) & = - 2 ( 1 ) ( - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + {1}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j} = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}\\end{array}$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3344/Source/Images/ebb132b3a8cdd98716106f9ef1c3b4a7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171822 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9734fc0-9789-5a9c-8ae0-806f87052875" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "8f9615ef-b53e-51ee-9aa6-4df88afd6773" :question/math-academy-id "e-9441" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3344/Source/Images/a781f1f2da9a61eb9baa82c2c759f6cf.png)\n\n1. $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf G(x,y) = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i}$\n3. $\\mathbf H(x,y) = -x \\, \\mathbf{i}$" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf j$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n$\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf i$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n$\\mathbf G(x,y) = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y) = -x\\, \\mathbf{i}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171826 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "b226c391-9538-59ff-85ff-dde4d73f209b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9473" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3344/Source/Images/f7b756645ce2d0592187333f7659de9e.png)\n\n1. $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k$\n2. $\\mathbf G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k$\n3. $\\mathbf H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k$\n4. $\\mathbf K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k$" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n$\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k$\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n$\\mathbf G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k$\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n$\\mathbf H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fa64aa7-5709-5a5b-8d86-fffb787a9837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x, y, z) = e^{x}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168488} {:answer/id #uuid "bbde0821-5ca8-5b14-9b26-42b2bd1117f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168489} {:answer/id #uuid "11e4e208-b631-5180-83d1-f903a669616e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x, y, z) = i + j + k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168490} {:answer/id #uuid "52cc7f27-742b-5dca-b965-b57b67eb0de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x, y, z) =-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168491} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd9f1b2-9fe3-5b3b-a277-cd832859bb20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x, y, z) = i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fd9f1b2-9fe3-5b3b-a277-cd832859bb20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x, y, z) = i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168492} :answer-field/id #uuid "902952fb-066e-5c9c-8874-81b4b024f4d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168487}] :question/id #uuid "505eb075-afb5-5225-b846-447773bcc89d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122559" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3344/Source/Images/q-122559.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "966275d1-af82-5a35-ad81-d04d70f7a2a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168500} {:answer/id #uuid "de6de102-1cb7-541a-ae2c-d752d13a95d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x, y, z) = 2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168501} {:answer/id #uuid "122a8d26-22cb-54a8-bb65-2abd1f512055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x, y, z) = \\ln xk" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168502} {:answer/id #uuid "67c29821-3f10-56d9-96b3-60fedc981778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x, y, z) = i - k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168503} {:answer/id #uuid "67e5e9a4-cb82-59f9-b4b5-137039129695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x, y, z) =-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de6de102-1cb7-541a-ae2c-d752d13a95d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x, y, z) = 2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168501} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7830737-52c7-561f-a08d-87ca04053e88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168499}] :question/id #uuid "0777fbfe-65b4-5afe-92ce-361fbbf5b37f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122664" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3344/Source/Images/q-122664.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?"}]}
